Yes. “Moving the goalposts” is one instance of a broader pattern: changing the game while people are still playing it.

Your framing generalizes it:

* Move the rules → what actions are allowed or rewarded changes.
    * New regulations.
    * New scoring system.
    * New interface.
    * New technology.
* Move the stakes → what matters changes.
    * Profit becomes growth.
    * Speed becomes safety.
    * Prestige becomes impact.
    * Winning one metric stops being sufficient.
* Move the quitting time → the optimization horizon changes.
    * Quarterly thinking becomes decade thinking.
    * Sprint becomes marathon.
    * “Ship now” becomes “maintain forever.”

There are also interactions between them:

Shift	Effect
Rules + Stakes	The same behavior can become good, bad, or irrelevant.
Rules + Time	A previously optimal strategy may become obsolete.
Stakes + Time	Patience or urgency suddenly dominates.
All three	It effectively becomes a new game, even if it looks familiar.

This suggests a higher-level operator:

Before optimizing, identify what game is actually being played—and whether someone has changed the rules, the stakes, or the horizon.

That operator applies well beyond games. It can be useful in business, negotiations, science, software, politics, and everyday relationships. People often argue because they’re implicitly playing different games: they may agree on the facts but disagree on the objective, the constraints, or the time horizon.

One extension is to distinguish between declared and actual games. Organizations and individuals sometimes state one set of rules, stakes, or horizons while rewarding another. Recognizing that mismatch can explain behavior that otherwise seems inconsistent.